      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics of managed conservation often overestimate human control over nature and underestimate the resilience required for complex biosystems to self-regulate. Their models typically rely on the flawed assumption that \"protected\" areas can be perfectly engineered to prevent all human encroachment, ignoring the reality that borders are permeable and perfect isolation is impossible to achieve. Ultimately, this one-size-fits-all approach is poorly conceived because it treats the workforce as the product rather than the toolbox, failing to recognize that the true work of our future will be the free and foraging of a rewilded, dynamic landscape.",
      "source": "target",
